Analysis complete. I read the whole tree (~19,200 lines across 43 files), cross-checked each subsystem with parallel deep dives, and verified the highest-impact claims directly. The project type-checks clean (zig build check), so these are design/robustness issues, not build failures.
Critical bugs (verified broken)
1. The reload_config keybinding never actually reloads.
utils.reload() only sets an atomic flag (src/core/utils/utils.zig:33-35) — it never writes to the signal pipe. The flag is consumed only inside the FD_SIGNAL poll branch (src/core/events.zig:373-377), so the .reload_config action (src/core/input/input.zig:262) only takes effect if some unrelated signal (usually a later SIGCHLD from an exec) happens to wake that branch. No signal → stale config forever. Fix: have reload() write a byte to the signal pipe, or poll consumeReload() unconditionally each loop iteration.
2. Xft.dpi detection is dead at startup — wrong init order.
main.zig:24 calls scale.detectDpi() before main.zig:40 calls utils.initAtomCache(). getAtomCached returns error.AtomCacheNotInitialized (src/core/utils/utils.zig:159-163), so the atom is always 0 and readXftDpi always fails (src/core/modules/scale.zig:29-32). The documented Xft.dpi precedence never fires; users with Xresources overrides get geometry-derived DPI instead. One-line fix: move initAtomCache above detectDpi — but also validate the Xft value first (it's fed unclamped straight into Pango at bar/drawing.zig:574).
3. The whole RandR refresh-rate path is dead code (~100 lines).
scale.zig:133-235 calls xcb_randr_* under @hasDecl(xcb, ...) guards, but the centralized cImport is only xcb/xcb.h (src/core/core.zig:10), xcb/randr.h is imported nowhere, and build.zig never links xcb-randr. Every call site always falls through to default_hz (60 Hz). The carousel's "auto-detect refresh rate" feature silently never works. Either add the header + link, or delete the block.
4. Multi-file config: duplicate scalar keys silently revert to defaults — the documented "later files win" contract is false.
config.zig:205-207 claims "Later files win on scalar conflicts; arrays accumulate", but mergeSectionsInto (src/config/parser.zig:290-311) accumulates every duplicate key into an array [v1, v2], and the typed getters (asInt/asBool/asString/asScalable, parser.zig:29-73) return null for .array. So master_count = 2 in one file + master_count = 3 in another merges to an array, the getter returns null, and the field silently falls back to its default. This breaks the primary use case for split configs. Also affects single-file duplicates: master_count = [5] (a one-element array) silently yields the default too. Fix: last-wins for scalars (keep array-accumulation only for genuinely array-typed keys like keybinds), and fix the doc comment.
5. workspaces.count is unbounded and can crash the WM.
Parsed as getInRange(u8, ..., 1, null) — no upper bound (src/config/config.zig:751); validate() only checks >= 1 (config.zig:313-316). But downstream is hard-capped at MAX_WORKSPACES = 64: tracking.zig:155-157 has a debug-only assert, and fullscreen.zig:44 has g_slots: [64]?FullscreenInfo indexed by raw workspace id (fullscreen.zig:109-111). count = 100 → startup panic in Debug, out-of-bounds read/write in ReleaseFast. Fix: pass MAX_WORKSPACES as the max to getInRange.
Correctness / robustness risks
6. Blocking X round-trips execute inside xcb_grab_server. setFocus always does a live blocking getInputModel reply (src/window/focus.zig:405), and is called under a server grab from new-window spawn (window.zig:979), all three unminimize paths (minimize.zig:135-308), workspace switch (workspaces.zig:659-689), and swap-master (input.zig:357-372). The codebase's own convention says round-trips must be hoisted above grabs (see fullscreen.zig:534-535, window.zig:1108-1114) — a mid-grab reply drain forces an implicit flush and defeats the grab's atomicity. Fix: pre-fire the cookie before the grab, or use cached accepts_input/take_focus inside grabs.
7. Bar redraw can flush inside a held server grab. scheduleFocusRedraw → drawTitleOnly → blitAndFlush (src/bar/bar.zig:1076-1087) is called from commitFocusTransition (focus.zig:356-357), which runs under grabs in switchToAll (workspaces.zig:410-423) and clearFocus (focus.zig:605). This is asymmetric with the guarded segmented-tick path (carousel.zig:315-322, which respects focus_signal.is_invalidated). Same class of bug as #6 — intermediate frame blitted inside the grab.
8. Event-dispatch table is indexable out-of-bounds. dispatch() computes idx = event_type & 0x7F into a 36-slot table (src/core/events.zig:93-96; constants.zig:147). Any extension event (types 64-127, e.g. XKB/XInput2/XFixes) is an OOB read. Unreachable today only because core events are selected exclusively — it's a latent memory-safety footgun the moment anyone subscribes to an extension. Guard event_type > XCB_GE_GENERIC before indexing.
9. Config reload is not atomic.
- Carousel options are written to module globals during parse, before validate() and the swap (config.zig:1188-1191 vs events.zig:255-263). A rejected reload silently applies the rejected config's carousel behavior.
- The errdefer new_config.deinit(...) at events.zig:259 stays armed across the swap; any future post-swap !void call would deinit the live config. Safe today only because every post-swap call is void.
- If bar.reload() fails during reload, the old bar keeps borrowing strings from old_config which is then freed at events.zig:272 → use-after-free on next draw.
10. Keyboard handling is frozen to startup state. The 256-entry keysym table is built once from the live XKB state (src/core/input/xkbcommon.zig:50-53), so CapsLock/NumLock on at startup silently corrupts every binding; XCB_MAPPING_NOTIFY (type 34) isn't dispatched, so setxkbmap/xmodmap changes never re-resolve. Also LOCK_MODIFIERS (constants.zig:138-143) omits ScrollLock/mod3 while input.zig:87-89 claims it's covered — with ScrollLock held, no grab matches. Fix: build the table from level-0 symbols and rebuild on mapping notify.
11. Config validation edge cases. master_width = 1.0 is accepted (config.zig:307-311) but renders the stack column off-screen (runtime clamps to 0.95, tiling.zig:38 — config and runtime disagree); conversely an absolute-pixel master_width = 60 fails the ratio check and rejects the entire config. And unknown action names silently become exec (config.zig:702-708) — a typo like togl_layout spawns a failing shell command.
12. MIN_WINDOW_DIM (50px) is enforced by some layouts but not others. master and scroll floor at 50px; grid, fibonacci, and leaf hand out arbitrarily small windows (e.g. leaf.zig:39-43 produces 26px halves). Same window count gives very different minimums per layout.
13. All config diagnostics are compiled out in the shipped build. The warn-and-revert design (config.zig:14-15) routes through debug.warn, and the whole debug module is gated on enable_debug_logging which is optimize == .Debug (build.zig, debug.zig:27-38) — while the preferred build is ReleaseFast. In production there is zero feedback for enabled = flase or an unknown key.
14. The optional bar can kill the WM. drawing.zig:602 has a bare @panic, plus asserts at bar.zig:983 and carousel.zig:525. Bar init is deliberately non-fatal (main.zig:52 catch), but a panic later takes the whole WM down.
15. Event-loop robustness. The drain loop while (xcb_poll_for_event) (events.zig:302) has no per-batch cap — a chatty client can starve signals/timers. Type-0 pseudo-events (X server errors from unchecked requests — the majority of sends are unchecked) map to dispatch_table[0] = null and vanish silently, making X11 failure diagnosis impossible.
Structure / big-picture
16. core.State is a mutable service locator. Every module imports core and reads/writes the global via getState() — which returns a *State that modules mutate directly (e.g. bar/bar.zig:1050 flips config.bar.bar_position). Ordering is comment-enforced only; any pre-init call hard-panics; config reload invalidates every pointer into the config held by other modules, so each must remember to rebuild (e.g. window.buildRulesMap at events.zig:277). No injection point → subsystems can't be unit-tested. The reload/UAF bugs above are direct consequences. Direction: pass an explicit state/config handle to subsystems, or funnel the swap through one function that rebuilds all borrowed views.
17. The "centralized" @cImport is duplicated. constants.zig:4 and input.zig:7-10 each do their own C import — the stated rationale for centralizing in core.zig:10 is defeated, and it's exactly why #3 (missing randr header) happened. Make constants.zig use core.xcb.
18. Tiling layout geometry is hand-duplicated across five modules. Each of master/grid/fibonacci/scroll/leaf re-derives gap/border inset math with only thin shared helpers (layouts.zig's outerArea/shrinkClamped), and they've already drifted on MIN_WINDOW_DIM (#12). Extract a shared distribute(region, count, gap, border) rect-splitting helper; keep only layout-specific ordering in each module.
19. prompt.zig + vim.zig = 2,642 lines, ~41% of the bar subsystem, with a circular import (prompt imports bar, bar imports prompt), a large @cImport block (prompt.zig:18-26), hand-declared xkb externs, and the Mode enum's integer value doubling as an index into a cached width array (vim.zig:33-38). These two files are the most likely to grow unboundedly and deserve splitting (key handling vs. completion/exec vs. state).
20. No bar widget abstraction. Every module hand-rolls draw(dc, config, height, start_x) !u16 with its own cache-invalidation scheme (tags invalidate, title title_cache, clock thread flag), and there are two separate drawSegment implementations (drawing.zig:403 and bar.zig:301). A shared widget protocol would remove most of the duplication the bar agent found.
21. utils.zig is a dumping ground — process atomics, geometry, atom cache, EWMH, scaling, clocks, grab/paint helpers, pipes, Mutex/Condition, BoundedList, property fetching in one 507-line file. Natural split: atoms.zig, sync.zig, geometry.zig, collections.zig.
22. Defaults are triplicated — types.zig struct defaults, getDefaultConfig (config.zig:352-364), and fallback.toml — with drift already visible ("master_left" vs "master-stack" vs "master"; master_width 50% vs 55%). getDefaultConfig's OOM fallback installs static literals that deinit later frees (config.zig:352 → types.zig:594-597) — free of non-heap memory on the OOM path. And buildConfigFromDoc (config.zig:374-386) leaks the whole partially-built config if any parse step errors — a missing errdefer.
23. Fragmented module-level globals. fullscreen.g_slots/g_float_saves, minimize.g_minimized, workspaces.g_state, tracking.g_*, focus.state, tiling.state all exist alongside core.State, each with its own duplicated getState()-style guard. Consistent and documented, but there's no teardown story and no isolation.
Localized / smaller items
- Title truncation is silent: fetchPropertyToBuffer caps at 256 bytes / doesn't validate reply type (utils.zig:486-507); max_visible_windows = 128 drops windows without notice (title.zig:26-28).
- WM_HINTS input-flag parsing is implemented twice (window.zig:296-309 and focus.zig:370-380) — will drift.
- transparency = 1 means 100% but indicator_padding = 1 means 1% — adjacent fields in [bar] with opposite interpretations.
- [binds] iterates a hashmap (config.zig:584) — duplicate-effective-bindings resolve nondeterministically.
- hz_cache (scale.zig:138) is a non-atomic global read from the carousel thread (safe today only by accident of ordering).
- Condition.initMonotonic casts a hardcoded 64-byte buffer to pthread_condattr_t (utils.zig:384-389) — silent stack corruption if any libc grows the type.
- main.zig:74 orelse unreachable on xcb_connect — the actual failure path is the has_error check below it, so drop the orelse.
- reapPendingChildren runs the spawn drain twice per SIGCHLD (events.zig:138-141 + input.zig:651).
- Drag state has no self-heal if a ButtonRelease is lost mid-drag (input.zig:169-183) — hover-focus is dropped until restart (acknowledged in the constants.zig:91-107 comment, but a DestroyNotify clear or timeout would close it).
Things done well (worth preserving)
- Ownership discipline around config strings (assignStr dupe-before-free, Document.deinit/Config.deinit split) — the parser/config ownership is mostly sound.
- The reload ordering for the success path (events.zig:251-280) is deliberate and correct.
- Input depth/bounds backstops (MAX_FILE_BYTES, MAX_ARRAY_DEPTH, MAX_KEYBIND_COOKIES).
- Helpful, self-documenting comments explaining why (the OFFSCREEN_X_POSITION, ROOT_WINDOW mask, and carousel guards in constants.zig).
